    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"240862\">@Oliver Nash</span> and <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"630477\">@Scott Carnahan</span> have thought about Lie algebras, I believe</p>",
        "id": 583831426,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1775493453
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I haven’t seen this kind of splitting structure on nilpotent Lie algebras before, and I’m pretty sure it hasn’t been done in Lean. However, this means I can’t really predict if it will be interesting or not. Edit: that said, it looks like an interesting area to explore, and if you find some useful API that is missing from mathlib, you should make a PR.</p>\n<p>Regarding the code you posted, I suggest you eliminate the <code>submoduleBracket</code> definition, and replace its use with more direct statements, e.g., the bracket product of any element of <code>V u</code> with an element of <code>V v</code> lies in <code>V (u + v)</code>. Graded Lie algebras have just landed in mathlib, so you may find some of that code convenient.</p>",
        "id": 583914065,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1775539203
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree with Scott's remarks, especially his second paragraph.</p>",
        "id": 583945974,
        "sender_full_name": "Oliver Nash",
        "timestamp": 1775552785
    }
